ope to try and figure out how  many viruses are there so how we do that  is you separate the bacteria and the  viruses from soil and then we take this  separated back  area and buyers full liquid and we pass  it through a point two micron filter so  what that does is that filter will allow  viruses and other small particles to be  separated from the larger cellular  community like the bacterial community  and we can centrifuge this we get  exclusively viral particles and then we  can stain these with a nucleic acid  stain and put them under the microscope  and we get something that looks about  like this so this is an image from our  two fantastic undergrads who have been  working on trying to make these  microscopic counts really robust and  repeatable and interestingly preliminary  numbers are showing we're getting  somewhere around 10 to the 7 viruses per  gram of soil which is a little bit on  the lower end but this is also sampled  at a point kind of later in this season  so this so it was a little drier there  might not be as much living in there so  this is exciting this is starting to  really allow us to connect the viral  counts or how many viruses are there to  the actual viral ecology and then what  I'm focusing on in my postdoc is taking  these viruses and further concentrating  them so that we can extract their DNA so  what this looks like is ultracentrifuge  and viruses to get them out of solution  for several hours and extracting their  DNA and sequencing that as well as we  look at and sequenced the total DNA of  the soil so this includes the bacteria  and the fungus and then we run all of  these sequences through a bioinformatic  pipeline that looks about like this so  once you have DNA and you sequence it  you get back using the technology we use  which is a woman of high seek sequencing  we get back DNA fragments that are about  125 to 150 base pairs long and that's  pretty short that's not always super  our informative about that length so  what we do to make more sense of this  data is we will assemble these fragments  in other words we will try to  reconstruct genes from these smaller  pieces of DNA and then from these  reconstructed genes we filter out the  sequences that are too short to be  confidently called as viruses and then  we do another check to ensure that all  of our sequences are from viruses and we  use a program called vs order to do that  and once we have all of our sequences  that we think are viral we can start  getting into the real science of it so  you can ask questions like who are these  viruses or who are these viruses  infecting we can ask questions about  their function so what are these viruses  doing do they have these extra metabolic  genes to tell their hosts to do  something for instance with the  glycoside hydrolases that were found in  the permafrost study and we can start to  look at relative abundances of viruses  so our by a specific type of virus more  abundant during the summer when fields  have Tomatoes actively growing  where are they more abundant during  winter when there's maybe not anything  growing in that field and some really  preliminary work that we have from some  of these tomato field soils that have  biochar in them is that there are quite  a few viruses in these soils so I'm  showing you here just a quick bar chart  with the different types of soil  amendments on the bottom so this  includes a control that's just normal  tomato soil or the normal soil that we  plant tomatoes in here in Northern  California Tech is soil that's been  amended with charred coconut shell pine  or almond shell and what we found from  this is that the number of viruses is  high and it's actually quite diverse so  we recovered over 3,000 viral  populations from over 6,000 viral  genomes so  that saying is we recovered about 6000  viral genomes and that about half of  those are unique within the dataset so  in addition to taking that study further  and really delving into who those  viruses are and what they're doing some  of the other work I'll be doing in my  postdoc coming up in the next year is  comparing viruses between organically  managed and conventionally or  traditionally managed soil so in  conventionally managed soil fields you  usually get some sort of fertilization  from like a liquid mineral fertilizer so  you get your nitrogen in the forms of  like nitrate ammonium like inorganic  forms and pesticides could be used for  pest management first it is and the  organic soils the fertilizer all comes  from chicken manure or from a cover crop  is planted in the winter like a lagoon  that will put nitrogen back into the  soil and some work out some of our  collaborators so it's done have already  shown that this will have changed on  microbial communities so we're  interested in this has changed the viral  community and justice have any impacts  on the functioning of the soil so how in  general chemicals are cycled in the soil  or in terms of how this could impact  plant health  so are there viruses that can either  promote plant health by say killing  plant pathogens or are there viruses  that are pathogenic to plants residing  in the soil and then within this will  also be comparing seasonality of soil  viral community so do these communities  change with this season and we suspect  that they do but stay tuned we'll get to  that and that is all I have to say for  today and I really want to thank you for  your time listening and I also want to  thank some of my friends house to glad  me it's a collaborator API dr. Emerson  are at UC Davis who have been  responsible for getting all the biochar  fields set up and running thank you Dr  zenki for that informative presentation  we will now start the Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar and we'll address some of the  most commonly asked questions by our  viewers if you have any questions you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question drop-down box  located on the far left of your  presentation window type your question  into the box it appears on the screen  and click the send button our speaker  will follow up with your questions via  email so let's get started our first  question is what are some of the major  limitations in viral ecology and how do  you address those yeah so like any field  new or well-established there are  several drawbacks that we really need to  consider when doing our studies I think  one of the most major ones is a problem  that we also have on micro ecology where  through studying anything without  culturing it we don't we're dependent on  things that have been cultivated to  understand what they're doing so as long  as we have for instance a gene that's  been previously studied then we can  compare what we find in our novel  systems and compare to what's been  previously studied and say okay we think  that this is doing this because they  look similar or they have similar  sequences but we also get a lot of DNA  sequences where we have no match to them  and those are going to be really hard to  figure out what they're doing just from  looking at a DNA sequence so that's when  we have to go back in to our samples and  try and recover those and actually  culture those so that's I think one of  the biggest drawbacks right now is we're  really limited in terms of what we  previously studied for comparing that to  what we currently are studying  and one of the other big drawbacks that  I'm having with my work is that since  viruses are so diverse in terms of if  their DNA based or RNA based and they  have different doubler single-stranded  nests it's hard to in one study target  all those different types of viruses so  I'm focusing mostly on DNA viruses right  now but for instance a lot of plant  viruses are RNA viruses so that's  something we're actively working on  being able to simultaneously examine but  there's still some methods development  that's going into that well thank you  for that next one is what are some of  the applications of soil viral ecology  so I think there are quite a few I think  they're all a little bit maybe a decade  off in the future but some of the things  that people are doing now that's really  exciting is trying to use viruses that  occur naturally to control pathogenic  plant pathogenic viruses that are sorry  um pathogenic bacteria and fungi and  things like nematodes and smaller or  naturally so that does not impact a plan  or a human and introducing it into an  environment to infect a fungus that does  negatively impact a plant is something  that's preliminary been really  successful in some sectors and there's a  lot of evidence that we can also do that  with things like mosquitoes or with  other pests including human pests and  that's why I think it's really important  to keep studying viral ecology since to  do something like that we also need to  know what are the other viruses in the  system and how will be impacting a whole  system so we don't accidentally you know  start a domino effect or anything very  interesting thank you for that next one  is our viral communities in soils  positive or negative for the environment  yeah so that's something I actually get  asked a lot so we  this view from including from a bunch of  really entertaining movies that viruses  are bad you know when you think of virus  do you think of something like the cold  or the flu and and I mean in humans and  pathogens  you know those viruses are bad but I  think what's going to happen with our  impression of viral ecology is it's  going to be similar to what's happened  in the past ten years with looking at  bacteria for instance in the human gut  or in agricultural soils where we know  that there's a small portion of these  entities bacterial or viral that aren't  good for us or for plants or something  like that but actually there are  probably a lot that are really  beneficial and critical to something  like nutrient cycling or controlling  populations of bacteria or other  organisms that keep something from you  know blooming and going out of control  and yeah so I think I mean it's  definitely going to be dependent on the  situation and the virus and what your  application is but I think in general  viral populations are a really  beneficial thing to have in the  environment good we have time for one  more question do human activities impact  soil viral ecology yeah so we don't know  yet  from some of the preliminary studies so  for instance the graphs I was showing  where there's different numbers of  viruses and different types of soil that  seems to indicate that in some cases we  might decrease the number of viruses  present in some of the soils so it looks  like we could have an impact on that we  also know from other systems so from  things like sewage or wastewater  treatment that humans can help to  increase the number of viruses in a  given place but again that's something  that we're not totally and hopefully  something bad through some of my work as  well as a working  a lot of a really fantastic scientists  well thank you I would like to once  again thank dr. Zinke for her  presentation I would also like to thank  lab roots for making today's educational  webcast possible before we go I want to  let everyone know that today's webcast  will be available for on-demand viewing  through December 13th 2018 you will  receive an email from laberd's letting  you know when this webcast will be  available for replay please share that  announcement with your colleagues who  may have missed today's event that is  all for now thank you for joining us  we hope to see you again soon bye
 

